#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050473 :topic/difficulty 1.1558263047756883 :topic/id #uuid "4c6d2580-78c9-5782-9c3a-465dcd055b32" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175922 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175735 :question/id #uuid "ca9ba6cf-860f-5868-b91f-1dff84406f22" :question/math-academy-id "e-14357" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that is perpendicular to $y = 4x - 1$ and passes through the point $(-8,7).$" :question/worked-solution "Let $l_1$ denote the line $y = 4x - 1,$ and let $l_2$ denote the line we want to find.\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of $l_2$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of $l_1.$ Since $l_1$ has a slope of $4,$ the line $l_2$ must have a slope of $-\\dfrac{1}{4}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\dfrac{1}{4}$ and pass through the point $(x_1,y_1)=(-8,7).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_1 &= m(x-x_1) \\\\[5pt]\ny - 7 &= -\\dfrac{1}{4}(x-(-8)) \\\\[5pt]\ny - 7 &= -\\dfrac{1}{4}(x+8)\\\\[5pt]\ny - 7 &= -\\dfrac{1}{4}x - 2 \\\\[5pt]\ny &= -\\dfrac{1}{4}x + 5\n\t\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ae4289af-e251-514c-981e-5c6d21992ad3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Perpendicular Line"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175923 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175737 :question/id #uuid "ca9ec8da-5f74-53dc-afa7-e6cc965fcc95" :question/math-academy-id "e-14363" :question/problem "Find the equation of the line that passes through the point $(3,4)$ and is perpendicular to the line passing through the points $(0,1)$ and $(-2,4).$" :question/worked-solution "Perpendicular lines have slopes which are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope $m_1$ of the line passing through the points $(0,1)$ and $(-2,4)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-1}{-2-0} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we want a perpendicular line, the slope $m$ of the line we're trying to find must satisfy\n\n$$\nm \\cdot \\left(-\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right) = -1 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad m = \\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nWe know that the line passes through the point $(3,4).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{2}{3}(x-3) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{2}{3}x-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{2}{3}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44b9b212-27c4-5d6d-a221-cd85c25fc956" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line Through Two Points"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175924 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175741 :question/id #uuid "49cee7e9-29a5-5e1e-bc13-7dbabaaea405" :question/math-academy-id "e-14730" :question/problem "What is the equation of the line that passes through the point $(6,2)$ and is perpendicular to $3x-2y=1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write the equation $3x-2y=1$ in its slope-intercept form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-2y & =1 \\\\ -2y & =-3x+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince perpendicular lines have slopes that are negative reciprocals of each other, the slope of the new line should be the negative reciprocal of the slope of the given line $y=\\dfrac{3}{2}x - \\dfrac{1}{2}.$ The given line has a slope of $\\dfrac{3}{2},$ so our perpendicular line must have a slope of $-\\dfrac{2}{3}.$\n\nOur perpendicular line must have the slope $m=-\\dfrac{2}{3}$ and pass through the point $(x_1,y_1) = (6,2).$ Using point-slope form and simplifying, we obtain the equation of the line in slope-intercept form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-6) \\\\ y-2 & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+4 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}x+6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c52fd5dd-780e-5dab-805f-561383873dd4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Line Perpendicular to a Line in Standard Form"}] :topic/math-academy-id 3562 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049119} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049564} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049583} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050409} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050667}] :topic/title "Finding Equations of Perpendicular Lines"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050711 :topic/difficulty 0.9795434122769167 :topic/id #uuid "608cf86b-83ea-5cc3-a6a8-222e7fa44e21" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064259 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064104 :question/id #uuid "fe999c48-1afd-5949-b4f9-533c09511d95" :question/math-academy-id "e-5295" :question/problem "Find the $x$-coordinate of the point on the curve $y=3x^2+1$ where the tangent line has a slope of $-1.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=3x^2+1.$ The slope of the tangent at the point $x$ is equal to $f'(x).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =3\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2x+0 \\\\  & =6x+0 \\\\  & =6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now set $f'(x) = -1$ and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-1 \\\\ 6x & =-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent is equal to $-1$ at the point where $x=-\\dfrac 1 6.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00a74f63-a4eb-5894-b4a4-eb4fd297a3f3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point on a Curve With a Certain Slope"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064342 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063980 :question/id #uuid "9c9b5972-f775-5cbe-8e66-2a0309f24f0e" :question/math-academy-id "e-142" :question/problem "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y = x^2 + \\dfrac {1}{x}$ at the point where $x=2.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=x^2+\\dfrac 1 x.$ The slope of the tangent at $x=2$ is equal to $f'(2).$ Computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}+{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot x-{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =2x-{x}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we evaluate at $x=2$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(2) & =2(2)-(2{)}^{-2} \\\\  & =4-\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve is $\\dfrac{15}{4}.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ffbc4bb-e179-543f-bcb5-3aaf6540a8a1" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of a Tangent Line of a Curve at a Given Point"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064343 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063983 :question/id #uuid "3138f267-3903-510c-86e8-5597d3689262" :question/math-academy-id "e-143" :question/problem "What is the slope of the tangent to the curve $y = x^4 + 3\\sqrt [3]{x^2}$ at the point $(1,4)?$" :question/worked-solution "This time, we'll use $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}$ notation. First, we compute the derivative, which gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4}+3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{4}+3{x}^{2/3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {x}^{3}+3\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}\\cdot {x}^{-1/3} \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}+2{x}^{-1/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=1$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & =4(1{)}^{3}+2(1{)}^{-1/3} \\\\  & =4+2 \\\\  & =6\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent to the curve is $6.$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ca13570-d504-53e6-8e02-0f1e85047a3e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of a Tangent Line of a Curve With Fractional Powers at a Given Point"}] :topic/math-academy-id 332 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048123} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050644}] :topic/title "Calculating the Slope of a Tangent Line Using Differentiation"}]}
